# Tabs

Parallel views of the same thing, switched in place — ARIA tab
markup auto-wired by basecoat's bundled JS.

<%= embed :tabs, :default %>

## When to use

- Views of one record the user switches between: summary /
  timeline / documents.
- Dense pages where parallel groupings genuinely compete for the
  same space.

## When not to use

- Stacked sections read in place — `accordion` (see the
  *Navigation* pattern page for the rule).
- Sequential steps — tabs imply free order; a wizard is a different
  pattern.
- Two tabs of which one is nearly empty — merge them.

## Usage

`render_tabs` (`Gesso::Components::TabsHelper`) renders the ARIA tab markup;
switching is auto-wired by basecoat's bundled JS.

```erb
<%%= render_tabs(
  id: "episode",
  tabs: [
    { label: "Summary",  content: "Episode summary." },
    { label: "Timeline", content: "What happened, when." }
  ]
) %>
```

| Param | Default | Notes |
| ----- | ------- | ----- |
| `id` | `"tabs"` | base for generated per-tab/panel ids |
| `tabs` | `[]` | `{ label:, content: }` hashes; the first tab starts selected |

Switching is handled by basecoat's bundled JS (self-registers on
`.tabs`, drives `aria-selected`/`aria-controls`) — no Stimulus
controller.

## Do and don't

- Do keep the set to ~5 tabs with one-or-two-word labels; tabs that
  wrap have stopped being tabs.
- Do put the most-used view first — it is the default.
- Don't make the active tab's content depend on another tab having
  been visited; every tab stands alone.
- Don't use tabs to hide required content — anything the user must
  see lives on the default tab or outside the tabs.
- Persisting the active tab in the URL (see *Navigation*: state in
  the URL) needs page-level wiring; raise it when a consuming page
  needs deep-linking rather than rebuilding the component.

## Accessibility and content

- Full ARIA tab pattern out of the box: `role="tablist"` /
  `tab` / `tabpanel`, arrow-key movement, roving tabindex — from
  the basecoat markup this partial renders.
- Labels are nouns naming the view ("Timeline"), never verbs.
